<ClinicNote slug="understand-your-pain">
  <h2>1. Pain is a protector, not a damage meter</h2>
  <p>Think of pain as your body's alarm system. Nerves in your tissues send danger signals, but they don't send pain. Your brain weighs those signals alongside everything else it knows, like past injuries, how tired or stressed you are, and what you believe is going on, then decides whether you need protecting. If it decides yes, you feel pain.</p>
  <p>That's why the world's pain scientists define pain as an experience, not an injury:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"An unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with, or resembling that associated with, actual or potential tissue damage."</p><cite>International Association for the Study of Pain, 2020 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <blockquote><p>"Pain cannot be inferred solely from activity in sensory neurons."</p><cite>IASP, notes on the definition [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>This explains everyday puzzles: a paper cut that hurts more than a deep gash you didn't notice until later, or a sore back that's worse on a stressful week.</p>
  <h2>2. Hurt doesn't always mean harm</h2>
  <p>After an injury, pain is helpful: it makes you protect the area while it heals. Most tissues heal within weeks to a few months. But sometimes the alarm stays switched on, and becomes more sensitive, after the tissue has healed. Doctors call pain lasting more than three months, or beyond the expected healing time, chronic or persistent pain [3]. About 1 in 5 Australians aged 45 and over live with it [4].</p>
  <p>With persistent pain, the system itself has become overprotective, like a smoke alarm that goes off when you make toast. The pain is completely real, but it's a less reliable sign of damage.</p>
  <h2>3. Scans show your age, not always your pain</h2>
  <p>Disc "degeneration" and bulges sound frightening, but they're extremely common in people with no pain at all. A review of scans from people without back pain found disc degeneration in 37% of 20-year-olds and 96% of 80-year-olds [5]. Think of them as wrinkles on the inside. That's why Australian guidelines say most back pain doesn't need a scan [2].</p>
  <h2>4. What turns the volume up, and down</h2>
  <p>Pain works like a volume dial, and many things besides your tissues can turn it. Sleep is a big one:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Sleep impairments are a stronger, more reliable predictor of pain than pain is of sleep impairments."</p><cite>Finan et al., Journal of Pain, 2013 [6]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>5. What the best research says helps</h2>
  <p>The strongest treatments for persistent pain combine understanding with action. In RESTORE, a large Australian trial of 492 people with long-term back pain, an approach that helped people make sense of their pain and rebuild confidence in movement produced improvements that lasted a year:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"CFT can produce large and sustained improvements for people with chronic disabling low back pain."</p><cite>Kent et al. (RESTORE trial), The Lancet, 2023 [7]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>In a US trial focused on retraining how the brain interprets back pain, 66% of people were pain-free or nearly pain-free after treatment, compared with 20% with placebo and 10% with usual care [8]. Learning about pain on its own has a modest effect; it works best combined with exercise [9]. The World Health Organization's guideline for chronic low back pain also recommends education, exercise programmes, and some hands-on therapies such as spinal manipulation and massage [10].</p>
  <h2>6. Five things you can start this week</h2>
  <ol class="cn-steps">
    <li><span><b>Keep moving, a little more each week.</b> Pick something you can do on a bad day and build from there. Gentle and regular beats heroic and occasional.</span></li>
    <li><span><b>Protect your sleep.</b> Consistent bed and wake times, a dark room, and less screen time before bed.</span></li>
    <li><span><b>Expect flare-ups, and have a plan.</b> A flare is a sensitive alarm, not new damage. Ease back, don't stop, then rebuild.</span></li>
    <li><span><b>Do things you enjoy.</b> Time with people, being outdoors, hobbies: they all turn the volume down.</span></li>
    <li><span><b>Ask questions until it makes sense.</b> Fear feeds pain. Understanding calms it.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <h2>How I can help</h2>
  <p>I'll listen to your whole story, examine you thoroughly, and explain what's going on in plain language. Then we build a plan together: hands-on treatment where it helps, exercise you can actually stick with, and the knowledge to manage flare-ups yourself. If something needs a doctor or another practitioner, I'll refer you.</p>

